split 5 balls into 2 boxes, where the order of the boxes doesn't matter.\n\nLet\u2019s denote the number of balls in each box as a pair $(a, b)$, where $a + b = 5$, and $a \\leq b$ (to avoid counting duplicates like $(2,3)$ and $(3,2)$).\n\nSo, the valid partitions are:\n\n- (0, 5)\n- (1, 4)\n- (2, 3)\n\nThese are all the **unordered** ways to split 5 into two non-negative integers.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Count the number of such partitions\n\nWe have found **3** distinct partitions:\n\n1. (0, 5)\n2. (1, 4)\n3. (2, 3)\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{3}\n$$\n\nThere are **3** ways to put 5 indistinguishable balls into 2 indistinguishable boxes.", "decode_seconds": 10.268725495319813, "draft_calls": 0, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 54, "method": "native_ar", "output_hash": "c15f2a7cf34ada463bd3b9fb585bd3fbcf9fa3c1eaa35712e2c856e5674a9e48", "output_tokens": 371, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 21755380736, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 22110273536, "problem_id": "math500/226", "profile_regions_ms": {"unattributed_runtime": 10302.57813166827}, "rank": 0, "reference_answer": "3", "repetition": 0, "request_seconds": 10.30257813166827, "reserved_memory_after_bytes": 22110273536, "reserved_memory_before_bytes": 22110273536, "target_calls": 371, "time_to_first_token_seconds": 0.03198481863364577, "turn_index": 0}
