{"acceptance_length": 5.716666666666667, "acceptance_lengths": [3, 6, 7, 5, 2, 10, 2, 5, 8, 3, 16, 7, 6, 2, 6, 2, 3, 1, 1, 1, 6, 1, 5, 7, 3, 5, 3, 4, 1, 8, 5, 1, 1, 3, 3, 2, 1, 11, 2, 9, 2, 6, 2, 5, 3, 1, 16, 10, 9, 11, 1, 16, 15, 16, 16, 8, 9, 9, 8, 3], "allocated_memory_after_bytes": 21690740224, "allocated_memory_before_bytes": 21690735104, "benchmark": "math500", "completion": "We are given:\n\n- **3 people** need to be assigned to **5 different offices**.\n- **Each person gets exactly one office**.\n- **No two people can share the same office** (i.e., no two people can be assigned to the same office).\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the problem\n\nWe are assigning **3 distinct people** to **5 distinct offices**, with the condition that **no two people share the same office**. That means:\n\n- Each person gets **one unique office**.\n- So, we are choosing **3 different offices** out of the 5, and assigning each person to one of them.\n\nThis is a **permutation** problem: we are choosing and arranging 3 offices out of 5 for the 3 people.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use the permutation formula\n\nThe number of ways to choose and arrange **r** items from **n** distinct items is given by the **permutation formula**:\n\n$$\nP(n, r) = \\frac{n!}{(n - r)!}\n$$\n\nHere:\n- $ n = 5 $ (total offices)\n- $ r = 3 $ (people to assign)\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nP(5, 3) = \\frac{5!}{(5 - 3)!} = \\frac{5!}{2!} = \\frac{120}{2} = 60\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{60}\n$$\n\nThere are **60** ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices, with no two people sharing the same office.", "decode_seconds": 2.4108791276812553, "draft_calls": 60, "event": "benchmark_row", "input_tokens": 58, "method": "native_target_dflash", "output_hash": "14be907d238eae95dbb1528efab89d03d6c556cd2f921ea21fabb014f164ec27", "output_tokens": 342, "peak_allocated_memory_bytes": 21800191488, "peak_reserved_memory_bytes": 22139633664, "problem_id": "math500/405", "profile_regions_ms": {"unattributed_ru